#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203707 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203885 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "004a102d-cfb9-539a-9fdd-11ca604fd7ec" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Transitive Relations"}] :question/id #uuid "c802160b-40ba-5bef-a32b-010ebdc4851f" :question/math-academy-id "e-14665" :question/problem "Consider the following relation defined on the set $A = \\{\\heartsuit,\\spadesuit,\\diamondsuit\\}.$ \n\n$$\nR = \\big\\{ (\\heartsuit,\\heartsuit), (\\heartsuit,\\spadesuit), (\\heartsuit,\\diamondsuit), (\\spadesuit,\\spadesuit), (\\spadesuit,\\diamondsuit), (\\diamondsuit, \\diamondsuit) \\big\\}\n$$\n\nDetermine whether the relation $R$ is transitive or not." :question/worked-solution "A binary relation $R$ defined on a set $A$ is *transitive* if, for all $x,y,z \\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y) \\in R \\quad\\textrm{and}\\quad (y,z) \\in R \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\quad (x,z) \\in R.\n$$\n\nIn other words, if the pairs $(x,y)$ and $(y,z)$ belong to a transitive relation, then the pair $(x,z)$ should also be in the relation.\n\nIt's sufficient to check all the \"linked\" pairs of the form $(x,y)$ with $x \\neq y$ and $(y,z)$ with $y \\neq z{:}$\n\n- Consider the pair $(\\heartsuit,\\spadesuit)$ whose second component is $\\spadesuit.$ It can be linked with the following pair from $R$ whose first component is $\\spadesuit{:}$ \n\n- Linking $(\\heartsuit,\\spadesuit)$ with $(\\spadesuit,\\diamondsuit)$ requires that $(\\heartsuit,\\diamondsuit) \\in R,$ and this pair is in $R.$ $\\:\\:{\\color{darkgreen}\\checkmark}$\n\n- Consider the pair $(\\heartsuit,\\diamondsuit)$ whose second component is $\\diamondsuit.$ It can't be linked with any pair from $R$ since none of them have a first component of $\\diamondsuit$ and a second component other than $\\diamondsuit.$\n\n- Consider the pair $(\\spadesuit,\\diamondsuit)$ whose second component is $\\diamondsuit.$ It can't be linked with any pair from $R$ since none of them have a first component of $\\diamondsuit$ and a second component other than $\\diamondsuit.$\n\nSo, for all $x,y,z \\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y) \\in R \\quad\\textrm{and}\\quad (y,z) \\in R \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\quad (x,z) \\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the relation is transitive."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203708 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203887 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f65dc026-1ec7-51f3-abc2-4a8ce95f9c2c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Transitive Relations From a Digraph"}] :question/id #uuid "7a9c02ab-eae3-52ee-9dd4-c81ca204ad4d" :question/math-academy-id "e-14666" :question/problem "Which of the following digraphs represents a transitive relation defined on the set $A = \\{1,2,3\\}?$\n\n![](images/f4/f4ad23f3c7794456a4cc01d4f801bfe4217605c5e17f1f612c5955be6bcaf6e6.png)" :question/worked-solution "A binary relation $R$ defined on a set $A$ is *transitive* if, for all $x,y,z \\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y) \\in R \\quad\\textrm{and}\\quad (y,z) \\in R \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\quad (x,z) \\in R.\n$$\n\nIn other words, if the pairs $(x,y)$ and $(y,z)$ belong to a transitive relation, then the pair $(x,z)$ should also be in the relation.\n\nIt's sufficient to check all the \"linked\" pairs of the form $(x,y)$ with $x \\neq y$ and $(y,z)$ with $y \\neq z.$\n\nIn terms of a digraph, if we have \"linked\" arrows $x \\rightarrow y$ and $y \\rightarrow z,$ then the arrow $x \\rightarrow z$ must be in the digraph too.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our digraphs.\n\n- Digraph I represents a transitive relation. There are no pairs of vertices for which $x \\rightarrow y$ and $y \\rightarrow z.$\n\n- Digraph II does not represent a transitive relation. It has arrows $1 \\rightarrow 2$ and $2 \\rightarrow 1,$ but has no arrow $1 \\rightarrow 1.$\n\n- Digraph III represents a transitive relation. For any pair of arrows $x \\rightarrow y$ and $y \\rightarrow z,$ the arrow  $x \\rightarrow z$ also belongs to the digraph.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203709 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203888 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "947f058c-4c47-52df-8c28-aed016d2b148" :knowledge-point/title "Transitive Relations Defined On Infinite Sets"}] :question/id #uuid "9e7589ef-e172-57ee-84f3-e085724db718" :question/math-academy-id "e-14667" :question/problem "Which of the following relations are transitive? \n\n- $x \\: R \\: y$ if and only if $x \\not\\mid y,$ defined on $\\mathbb{Z}$\n\n- $x \\: S \\: y$ if and only if $x = y,$ defined on $\\mathbb{R}$\n\n- $x \\: T \\: y$ if and only if $6 \\mid (x+y),$ defined on $\\mathbb{Z}$" :question/worked-solution "A binary relation $R$ defined on a set $A$ is *transitive* if, for all $x,y,z \\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y) \\in R \\quad\\textrm{and}\\quad (y,z) \\in R \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\quad (x,z) \\in R.\n$$\n\nIn other words, if the pairs $(x,y)$ and $(y,z)$ belong to a transitive relation, then the pair $(x,z)$ should also be in the relation.\n\nIt's sufficient to check all the \"linked\" pairs of the form $(x,y)$ with $x \\neq y$ and $(y,z)$ with $y \\neq z.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our relations.\n\n- Relation $R$ is not transitive. Notice that \n\n- $(2,3) \\in R$ since $2 \\not\\mid 3,$ and\n\n- $(3,4) \\in R$ since $3 \\not\\mid 4,$ but\n\n- $(2,4) \\notin R$ since $2 \\mid 4.$\n\n- Relation $S$ is transitive. Indeed, if $(x,y) \\in S$ and $(y,x) \\in S,$ then $(x,z) \\in S.$ Notice that \n\n$$\nx = y \\quad\\textrm{and}\\quad y = z \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad x = z\n$$\n\nfor all $x,y,z \\in \\mathbb{R}.$\n\n- Relation $T$ is not transitive. Notice that \n\n- $(2,4) \\in T$ since $6 \\mid (2+4),$ and\n\n- $(4,8) \\in T$ since $6 \\mid (4+8),$ but\n\n- $(2,8) \\notin T$ since $6 \\not\\mid (2+8).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$S$ only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203718 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203886 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "630cd12d-1141-51ae-8f2e-a881622868e8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Transitive Relations in Special Cases"}] :question/id #uuid "b2641213-6efe-5412-a585-9cb348ab00b0" :question/math-academy-id "e-14701" :question/problem "Which of the following relations on the set $A = \\{\\alpha, \\beta, \\delta\\}$ are transitive?\n\n- $R_1 = A^2$\n\n- $R_2 = \\{(\\alpha,\\beta), (\\alpha,\\delta)\\}$\n\n- $R_3 = \\{(\\beta,\\alpha), (\\beta,\\delta), (\\delta, \\beta)\\}$\n\n- $R_4 = \\{(\\alpha,\\alpha), (\\beta,\\beta), (\\delta, \\delta)\\}$" :question/worked-solution "A binary relation $R$ defined on a set $A$ is *transitive* if, for all $x,y,z \\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y) \\in R \\quad\\textrm{and}\\quad (y,z) \\in R \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\quad (x,z) \\in R.\n$$\n\nIn other words, if the pairs $(x,y)$ and $(y,z)$ belong to a transitive relation, then the pair $(x,z)$ should also be in the relation.\n\nIt's sufficient to check all the \"linked\" pairs of the form $(x,y)$ with $x \\neq y$ and $(y,z)$ with $y \\neq z{:}$\n\nMoreover, we recall the following:\n\n- The universal relation $R = A^2$ is transitive since it includes all possible ordered pairs.\n\n- The empty relation $R = \\emptyset$ is vacuously transitive since the antecedent in the implication is always false, and therefore the implication is always true.\n\n- A relation containing no \"linked\" pairs is always transitive.\n\n- A relation containing only reflexive pairs is always transitive.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our relations:\n\n- $R_1$ is transitive since it's the universal relation. $\\:\\:{\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n\n- $R_2$ is transitive since it contains no \"linked\" pairs.  $\\:\\:{\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n\n- $R_3$ is *not* transitive. Linking $(\\beta,\\delta)$ with $(\\delta, \\beta)$ requires that $(\\beta,\\beta) \\in R_3.$ However, this pair is *not* in $R_3.$ $\\:\\:{\\color{red}\\times}$\n\n- $R_4$ is transitive since it contains only reflexive pairs.  $\\:\\:{\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and IV only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204265 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203885 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "004a102d-cfb9-539a-9fdd-11ca604fd7ec" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Transitive Relations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7904eac5-67cc-5543-b04c-b12a6cba626b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "transitive" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418186} {:answer/id #uuid "b050aaf0-ce42-5a7f-8408-6f48f8380a1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "not transitive" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418202}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7904eac5-67cc-5543-b04c-b12a6cba626b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "transitive" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418186} :answer-field/id #uuid "0503ba64-6251-5d0b-868d-6f53851f7698" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418222} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "93f12709-ccd0-51d5-ab47-ae9f71fda533" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\notin " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418192} {:answer/id #uuid "f4d1a090-14d3-53f7-a770-9ccb45f3fec3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\in " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418219}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f4d1a090-14d3-53f7-a770-9ccb45f3fec3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\in " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418219} :answer-field/id #uuid "128eeed9-769b-58c6-9e3f-3255a669e8b9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418224} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5f3c9782-6a8a-579e-ba08-676802087c78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(z,x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418182} {:answer/id #uuid "79b30ad4-1bda-57c8-83f0-1c3468215f54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(z,z)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418187} {:answer/id #uuid "828eca8b-e332-5a57-bed1-3a0700847f74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x,x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418189} {:answer/id #uuid "abee7220-17cb-5dd0-94c2-33084d39e403" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x,z)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418200} {:answer/id #uuid "f5c34013-02d9-5d2d-ae31-f292680b29b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y,y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418220}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "abee7220-17cb-5dd0-94c2-33084d39e403" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x,z)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418200} :answer-field/id #uuid "18473a2f-39a3-525e-b6d3-37aafd0009ed" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418225} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "094ca2dc-5a28-5bd5-982c-a8839e78c987" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\notin " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418161} {:answer/id #uuid "0d89559c-8b93-5a56-8be7-a62e839644a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\in " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418163}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0d89559c-8b93-5a56-8be7-a62e839644a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\in " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418163} :answer-field/id #uuid "19b909e7-859c-5d54-9d15-aec04dd7113f" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418227} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3144fdfd-1612-56e5-a89f-1e8bc6c495fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(8,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418171} {:answer/id #uuid "36d738e0-5f31-50b1-8918-aba63fd1c1c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418174} {:answer/id #uuid "73560a46-39e0-5eb3-85e9-338e3894a509" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418185} {:answer/id #uuid "882d8c7f-a581-579f-a530-de35b09f833c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(8,8)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418190} {:answer/id #uuid "b7d20a5c-de38-52bd-a2e1-c3df98b7b782" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,8)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418205}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b7d20a5c-de38-52bd-a2e1-c3df98b7b782" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,8)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418205} :answer-field/id #uuid "ef4800a4-4dd7-502d-8ed6-b7f58d1fc8fb" :answer-field/key "field-5" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418242}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9a0247d0-2902-5cf0-983e-f6aaa39592e8" :question/math-academy-id "q-213430" :question/problem "Consider the following relation defined on the set $A={2,4,8}.$ \n\n$$\nR={(2,2),(2,4),(2,8),(4,2),(4,4),(4,8)}\n$$\n\nSelect the options that make the following reasoning correct:\n\nIn this relation, for all $x,y,z\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y){{field-1}}R\\,\\text{and}\\,(y,z){{field-2}}R\\,⟹\\,{{field-3}}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the relation is \n{{field-4}}.\n\nFor example, since $(2,4)$ and $(4,8)$ belong to $R,$ transitivity of $R$ requires \n{{field-5}}\n$\\in R.$" :question/worked-solution "A binary relation $R$ defined on a set $A$ is *transitive* if, for all $x,y,z\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,\\text{and}\\,(y,z)\\in R\\,⟹\\,(x,z)\\in R.\n$$\n\nIn other words, if the pairs $(x,y)$ and $(y,z)$ belong to a transitive relation, then the pair $(x,z)$ should also be in the relation.\n\nIt's sufficient to check all the \"linked\" pairs of the form $(x,y)$ with $x\\ne y$ and $(y,z)$ with $y\\ne z:$\n\n- Consider the pair $(2,4)$ whose second component is $4.$ It can be linked with the following pairs from $R$ whose first component is $4:$ \n\n- Linking $(2,4)$ with $(4,2)$ requires that $(2,2)\\in R,$ and this pair is in $R.$ $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Linking $(2,4)$ with $(4,8)$ requires that $(2,8)\\in R,$ and this pair is in $R.$ $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Consider the pair $(2,8)$ whose second component is $8.$ It can't be linked with any pair from $R$ since none of them have a first component of $8.$\n\n- Consider the pair $(4,2)$ whose second component is $2.$ It can be linked with the following pairs from $R$ whose first component is $2:$ \n\n- Linking $(4,2)$ with $(2,4)$ requires that $(4,4)\\in R,$ and this pair is in $R.$ $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Linking $(4,2)$ with $(2,8)$ requires that $(4,8)\\in R,$ and this pair is in $R.$ $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Consider the pair $(4,8)$ whose second component is $8.$ It can't be linked with any pair from $R$ since none of them have a first component of $8.$\n\nSo, for all $x,y,z\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,\\text{and}\\,(y,z)\\in R\\,⟹\\,(x,z)\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the relation is  $\\text{transitive}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204266 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203885 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "004a102d-cfb9-539a-9fdd-11ca604fd7ec" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Transitive Relations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11527144-b995-5941-ba03-c4baf29af809" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418165} {:answer/id #uuid "a240eaa0-782f-5f23-ae2b-03fdf9beab76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418197}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "11527144-b995-5941-ba03-c4baf29af809" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418165} :answer-field/id #uuid "23946d69-9f9f-5316-a125-b5686b969fb7" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418230} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01d07d82-bd5f-595d-b025-e9ac237f4946" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418160} {:answer/id #uuid "59f773e3-8450-5f0d-8604-aafc7e95bffc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418180}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "59f773e3-8450-5f0d-8604-aafc7e95bffc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418180} :answer-field/id #uuid "8f053f47-0fb2-59a6-a9af-f0fec0e41d77" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418234} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4e0858b5-c929-5bd0-afda-32b8314adc43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418177} {:answer/id #uuid "cad2ecc4-a926-5813-a1cb-308c6ba13227" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418212}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4e0858b5-c929-5bd0-afda-32b8314adc43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418177} :answer-field/id #uuid "cf8c9528-4b68-5e94-9cc1-f08494869ea5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418239} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c4cd3338-7c8f-57cd-acd4-a846dbc5dc7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "transitive" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418209} {:answer/id #uuid "d2acfe4c-3493-5b61-80ea-d1e232568847" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "not transitive" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418214}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d2acfe4c-3493-5b61-80ea-d1e232568847" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "not transitive" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418214} :answer-field/id #uuid "ee888080-eb0a-5b7f-97aa-81b36e4d160c" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418241}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4470ad24-c221-5b90-921e-3633822b45db" :question/math-academy-id "q-213431" :question/problem "Consider the following relation defined on the set $A={1,2,3}.$ \n\n$$\nR={(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,3),(3,2),(3,3)}\n$$\n\nSelect the options that make the following reasoning correct:\n\nSince \n{{field-1}}\n$\\in R$ and \n{{field-2}}\n$\\in R,$ but \n{{field-3}}\n$\\notin R,$ the relation is\n{{field-4}}." :question/worked-solution "A binary relation $R$ defined on a set $A$ is *transitive* if, for all $x,y,z\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,\\text{and}\\,(y,z)\\in R\\,⟹\\,(x,z)\\in R.\n$$\n\nIn other words, if the pairs $(x,y)$ and $(y,z)$ belong to a transitive relation, then the pair $(x,z)$ should also be in the relation.\n\nIt's sufficient to check all the \"linked\" pairs of the form $(x,y)$ with $x\\ne y$ and $(y,z)$ with $y\\ne z:$\n\n- Consider the pair $(1,2)$ whose second component is $2.$ It can be linked with the following pairs from $R$ whose first component is $2:$ \n\n- Linking $(1,2)$ with $(2,1)$ requires that $(1,1)\\in R,$ and this pair is in $R.$ $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Linking $(1,2)$ with $(2,3)$ requires that $(1,3)\\in R,$ and this pair is in $R.$ $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Consider the pair $(1,3)$ whose second component is $3.$ It can be linked with the following pairs from $R$ whose first component is $3:$ \n\n- Linking $(1,3)$ with $(3,2)$ requires that $(1,2)\\in R,$ and this pair is in $R.$ $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Consider the pair $(2,1)$ whose second component is $1.$ It can be linked with the following pairs from $R$ whose first component is $1:$ \n\n- Linking $(2,1)$ with $(1,2)$ requires that $(2,2)\\in R.$ However, this pair is *not* in $R.$ $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nWe can stop checking since we have found a chain of pairs that do not satisfy the transitivity condition. Therefore, we can conclude the following:\n\nSince $(2,1)\\in R$ and $(1,2)\\in R$ but $(2,2)\\notin R,$ the relation is  $\\text{not transitive}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418245 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203888 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "947f058c-4c47-52df-8c28-aed016d2b148" :knowledge-point/title "Transitive Relations Defined On Infinite Sets"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c2958ba5-f4a7-5209-8df3-1f6cb1c7c4c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,1)\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418208} {:answer/id #uuid "f933ce82-e344-5cf6-a182-c384a6e0a795" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,-2)\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418221}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f933ce82-e344-5cf6-a182-c384a6e0a795" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,-2)\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418221} :answer-field/id #uuid "1f1e0f09-949f-54d6-931b-a8acdad7849c" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418229} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f2f48f7-907c-5201-9e82-8318cf32ee9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "transitive" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418164} {:answer/id #uuid "9d312a08-bec8-54ed-928e-fb44841278d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "not transitive" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418196}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9d312a08-bec8-54ed-928e-fb44841278d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "not transitive" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418196} :answer-field/id #uuid "4eda63be-0db3-597c-99cd-8d04a3169f0d" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418232} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c81ee74-4165-534e-af15-27d266abcfea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,3)\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418162} {:answer/id #uuid "6041ca23-bc67-5932-802e-ab03ef5d1b6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,2)\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418183}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0c81ee74-4165-534e-af15-27d266abcfea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,3)\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418162} :answer-field/id #uuid "b6d06f9b-e36a-58c5-96a2-91bce44b80cf" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418235} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20f9e528-a322-5fab-b404-6979d866a63b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,1)\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418166} {:answer/id #uuid "35e7dbdc-f004-543a-93fd-daec3d693af1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,-2)\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418173} {:answer/id #uuid "43b1f671-c4ee-5c91-9ebe-8a00a2ddd2c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,1)\\notin R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418175} {:answer/id #uuid "949b1d87-d93b-501e-aa04-48eb488ae07a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,-2)\\notin R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418193}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "949b1d87-d93b-501e-aa04-48eb488ae07a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,-2)\\notin R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418193} :answer-field/id #uuid "c438305c-c60f-5ca3-9fe4-64720823e89a" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418237}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "55431de3-ba18-5b2d-9929-1fa00aa2cc43" :question/math-academy-id "q-213994" :question/problem "Let $R$ be a relation defined on the set $R$ as follows: \n\n$x\\,R\\,y$ if and only if $x+y=1$\n\nSelect the options that make the following reasoning correct:\n\nSince \n{{field-1}}\nand \n{{field-2}},\nbut \n{{field-3}},\nthe relation is \n{{field-4}}." :question/worked-solution "A binary relation $R$ defined on a set $A$ is *transitive* if, for all $x,y,z\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,\\text{and}\\,(y,z)\\in R\\,⟹\\,(x,z)\\in R.\n$$\n\nIn other words, if the pairs $(x,y)$ and $(y,z)$ belong to a transitive relation, then the pair $(x,z)$ should also be in the relation.\n\nIt's sufficient to check all the \"linked\" pairs of the form $(x,y)$ with $x\\ne y$ and $(y,z)$ with $y\\ne z.$\n\nFor our relation, notice that\n\n- $(-2,3)\\in R$ since $-2+3=1,$ and\n\n- $(3,-2)\\in R$ since $3+(-2)=1,$ but\n\n- $(-2,-2)\\notin R$ since $-2+(-2)\\ne 1.$\n\nTherefore, we can conclude the following:\n\nSince $(-2,3)\\in R$ and $(3,-2)\\in R$ but \n$(-2,-2)\\notin R,$ the relation is $\\text{not transitive}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418247 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203888 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "947f058c-4c47-52df-8c28-aed016d2b148" :knowledge-point/title "Transitive Relations Defined On Infinite Sets"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9c5a523e-87ad-564c-a53c-0f5976b548cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$S$ and $T$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418195} {:answer/id #uuid "c19f5713-624c-5de7-b871-ec4d7fff4f4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$S$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418207} {:answer/id #uuid "c9cc9a32-4374-5c62-b18b-114a993cc029" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$T$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418211} {:answer/id #uuid "e44c0d0e-d054-5dd5-8926-ef5982032103" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$R$ and $T$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418217} {:answer/id #uuid "e564fc12-fb2c-519f-9637-195e019015f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$R$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418218}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e44c0d0e-d054-5dd5-8926-ef5982032103" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$R$ and $T$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418217} :answer-field/id #uuid "edb2118f-fc60-559a-b8bd-52ea337c03d8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418240}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "70eb9024-0964-59ad-a103-f6948d25a78c" :question/math-academy-id "q-214002" :question/problem "Which of the following relations are transitive? \n\n- $x\\,R\\,y$ if and only if $x∣y,$ defined on $Z$\n\n- $x\\,S\\,y$ if and only if $x+y<4,$ defined on $R$\n\n- $x\\,T\\,y$ if and only if $x≡y\\,(\\text{mod}\\,4),$ defined on $Z$" :question/worked-solution "A binary relation $R$ defined on a set $A$ is *transitive* if, for all $x,y,z\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,\\text{and}\\,(y,z)\\in R\\,⟹\\,(x,z)\\in R.\n$$\n\nIn other words, if the pairs $(x,y)$ and $(y,z)$ belong to a transitive relation, then the pair $(x,z)$ should also be in the relation.\n\nIt's sufficient to check all the \"linked\" pairs of the form $(x,y)$ with $x\\ne y$ and $(y,z)$ with $y\\ne z.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our relations.\n\n- Relation $R$ is transitive. Indeed, if $(x,y)\\in R$ and $(y,x)\\in R,$ then $(x,z)\\in R.$ Notice that \n\n$$\nx∣y\\,\\text{and}\\,y∣z\\,⟹\\,x∣z\n$$\n\nfor all $x,y,z\\in Z.$\n\n- Relation $S$ is not transitive. Notice that \n\n- $(1,-4)\\in S$ since $1+(-4)=-3<4,$ and\n\n- $(-4,5)\\in S$ since $-4+5=1<4,$ but\n\n- $(1,5)\\notin S$ since $1+5=6≮4.$\n\n- Relation $T$ is transitive. Indeed, if $(x,y)\\in T$ and $(y,x)\\in T,$ then $(x,z)\\in T.$ Notice that \n\n$$\nx≡y\\,(\\text{mod}\\,4)\\,\\text{and}\\,y≡z\\,(\\text{mod}\\,4)\\,⟹\\,x≡z\\,(\\text{mod}\\,4)\n$$\n\nfor all $x,y,z\\in Z.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$R$ and $T$ only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418248 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203887 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f65dc026-1ec7-51f3-abc2-4a8ce95f9c2c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Transitive Relations From a Digraph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2eb95cc2-4351-5219-bf66-5f1e95ed104c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\to 4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418169} {:answer/id #uuid "4e1c3e39-8891-537f-a591-b967bd41d83a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\to 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418178}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4e1c3e39-8891-537f-a591-b967bd41d83a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\to 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418178} :answer-field/id #uuid "1e33ceb2-527d-539c-aac7-2b5c809de84e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418228} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b6037192-7f93-5c5f-b0b2-e7993fdde29a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\to 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418204} {:answer/id #uuid "df66e2d9-8d32-51de-a62f-82de35f4f3d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\to 4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418216}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "df66e2d9-8d32-51de-a62f-82de35f4f3d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\to 4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418216} :answer-field/id #uuid "3cbb7d3e-9fb9-5ba5-8426-510de8de7d0c" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418231} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "899fb552-6af3-5ceb-bf2a-62dbe05b5878" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "not transitive" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418191} {:answer/id #