y relation $R$ defined on a set $A$ is *transitive* if, for all $x,y,z\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,\\text{and}\\,(y,z)\\in R\\,⟹\\,(x,z)\\in R.\n$$\n\nIn other words, if the pairs $(x,y)$ and $(y,z)$ belong to a transitive relation, then the pair $(x,z)$ should also be in the relation.\n\nIt's sufficient to check all the \"linked\" pairs of the form $(x,y)$ with $x\\ne y$ and $(y,z)$ with $y\\ne z.$\n\nIn terms of a digraph, if we have \"linked\" arrows $x\\to y$ and $y\\to z,$ then the arrow $x\\to z$ must be in the digraph too.\n\nExamining the diagram, we conclude the following:\n\nSince the digraph has arrows $1\\to 2$ and $2\\to 4$ but has no arrow $1\\to 4,$ the relation is $\\text{not transitive}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418246 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203887 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f65dc026-1ec7-51f3-abc2-4a8ce95f9c2c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Transitive Relations From a Digraph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4e69dd30-577b-5eb0-8abc-73d0336ecd72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418179} {:answer/id #uuid "81acb1cb-213f-53e4-b5af-c60b8dbbec53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418188} {:answer/id #uuid "9bc6d790-c4ad-5910-942b-7e8326e0da7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418194} {:answer/id #uuid "a9c59538-8b1e-5b20-9482-690ba54c57e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418199} {:answer/id #uuid "c5c12167-746e-5442-b291-b883d8a00d11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418210}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9bc6d790-c4ad-5910-942b-7e8326e0da7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418194} :answer-field/id #uuid "18915ff6-afcd-56a1-8b11-45e467d4f091" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418226}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "64ea6724-f6be-5851-a399-860e0e1924d8" :question/math-academy-id "q-214065" :question/problem "![](images/1f/1fa323b54736f62bb334ae491d8d41deeba4a4e325f6e56f9651da147d50c349.png)\n\nWhich of the above digraphs represents a transitive relation defined on the set $A={p,q,r}?$" :question/worked-solution "A binary relation $R$ defined on a set $A$ is *transitive* if, for all $x,y,z\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,\\text{and}\\,(y,z)\\in R\\,⟹\\,(x,z)\\in R.\n$$\n\nIn other words, if the pairs $(x,y)$ and $(y,z)$ belong to a transitive relation, then the pair $(x,z)$ should also be in the relation.\n\nIt's sufficient to check all the \"linked\" pairs of the form $(x,y)$ with $x\\ne y$ and $(y,z)$ with $y\\ne z.$\n\nIn terms of a digraph, if we have \"linked\" arrows $x\\to y$ and $y\\to z,$ then the arrow $x\\to z$ must be in the digraph too.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our digraphs.\n\n- Digraph I does not represent a transitive relation. It has arrows $q\\to r$ and $r\\to p,$ but has no arrow $q\\to p.$\n\n- Digraph II represents a transitive relation. For any pair of arrows $x\\to y$ and $y\\to z,$ the arrow  $x\\to z$ also belongs to the digraph.\n\n- Digraph III does not represent a transitive relation. It has arrows $p\\to q$ and $q\\to r,$ but has no arrow $p\\to r.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418243 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203886 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "630cd12d-1141-51ae-8f2e-a881622868e8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Transitive Relations in Special Cases"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2571c089-3c52-59d7-b473-6f9e0305f21e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418167} {:answer/id #uuid "2f967206-082f-5b15-9867-3bb8d435bad0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, III, and IV" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418170} {:answer/id #uuid "ac9e3534-044f-5762-90a9-b43cb8e6b482" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418201} {:answer/id #uuid "cf9cc960-4433-54c6-935f-f6a98ffc3777" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418213} {:answer/id #uuid "db2ad807-5ae0-5e39-8f4e-ad108c836dcd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418215}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2f967206-082f-5b15-9867-3bb8d435bad0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, III, and IV" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418170} :answer-field/id #uuid "c8488c7a-8619-58cd-a22e-a5aa6766ebf6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418238}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "202c6abf-dfb5-522a-9256-456835ce12bd" :question/math-academy-id "q-214430" :question/problem "Which of the following relations on the set $A={1,3,5,7}$ are transitive?\n\n- ${R}_{1}=∅$\n- ${R}_{2}={A}^{2}$\n- ${R}_{3}={(5,5)}$\n- ${R}_{4}={(5,1),(7,3)}$" :question/worked-solution "A binary relation $R$ defined on a set $A$ is *transitive* if, for all $x,y,z\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,\\text{and}\\,(y,z)\\in R\\,⟹\\,(x,z)\\in R.\n$$\n\nIn other words, if the pairs $(x,y)$ and $(y,z)$ belong to a transitive relation, then the pair $(x,z)$ should also be in the relation.\n\nIt's sufficient to check all the \"linked\" pairs of the form $(x,y)$ with $x\\ne y$ and $(y,z)$ with $y\\ne z:$\n\nMoreover, we recall the following:\n\n- The universal relation $R={A}^{2}$ is transitive since it includes all possible ordered pairs.\n\n- The empty relation $R=∅$ is vacuously transitive since the antecedent in the implication is always false, and therefore the implication is always true.\n\n- A relation containing no \"linked\" pairs is always transitive.\n\n- A relation containing only reflexive pairs is always transitive.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our relations:\n\n- ${R}_{1}$ is transitive since it's the empty relation. $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- ${R}_{2}$ is transitive since it's the universal relation. $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- ${R}_{3}$ is transitive since it contains only reflexive pairs.  $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- ${R}_{4}$ is transitive since it contains no \"linked\" pairs.  $\\,\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, III, and IV.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418244 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203886 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "630cd12d-1141-51ae-8f2e-a881622868e8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Transitive Relations in Special Cases"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "344c8bff-5d19-596d-a2bf-472abe96d0ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418172} {:answer/id #uuid "5b9a150a-cc66-599e-963e-2b2c4bed2295" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418181} {:answer/id #uuid "62c920c8-4114-5cee-ab28-e34c74683438" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418184} {:answer/id #uuid "b26882ef-7ad2-53e9-a273-98e2183e198c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418203} {:answer/id #uuid "bf74cb06-4c45-5759-8ad8-f5a6d7a6e7f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, III, and IV" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418206}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bf74cb06-4c45-5759-8ad8-f5a6d7a6e7f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, III, and IV" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418206} :answer-field/id #uuid "0ba6de5b-5a73-5a97-aff3-85fcde63e486" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418223}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "44cd3428-3b1c-5e19-9525-5e86c3ca616a" :question/math-academy-id "q-214431" :question/problem "Which of the following relations on the set $A={♢,♡,♠,♣}$ are transitive?\n\n- ${R}_{1}={(♢,♢),(♣,♣)}$\n- ${R}_{2}={(♠,♠)}$\n- ${R}_{3}={(♢,♠),(♡,♣)}$\n- ${R}_{4}=∅$" :question/worked-solution "A binary relation $R$ defined on a set $A$ is *transitive* if, for all $x,y,z\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,\\text{and}\\,(y,z)\\in R\\,⟹\\,(x,z)\\in R.\n$$\n\nIn other words, if the pairs $(x,y)$ and $(y,z)$ belong to a transitive relation, then the pair $(x,z)$ should also be in the relation.\n\nIt's sufficient to check all the \"linked\" pairs of the form $(x,y)$ with $x\\ne y$ and $(y,z)$ with $y\\ne z:$\n\nMoreover, we recall the following:\n\n- The universal relation $R={A}^{2}$ is transitive since it includes all possible ordered pairs.\n\n- The empty relation $R=∅$ is vacuously transitive since the antecedent in the implication is always false, and therefore the implication is always true.\n\n- A relation containing no \"linked\" pairs is always transitive.\n\n- A relation containing only reflexive pairs is always transitive.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our relations:\n\n- ${R}_{1}$ is transitive since it contains only reflexive pairs.  $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- ${R}_{2}$ is transitive since it contains only reflexive pairs.  $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- ${R}_{3}$ is transitive since it contains no \"linked\" pairs.  $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- ${R}_{4}$ is transitive since it's the empty relation. $\\,\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, III, and IV.\""}]}
